# Limitations

Known, deliberate limitations and exceptions to the project's own rules -
what a user or a contributor should know before relying on a result. Each
entry: what it is, why it is accepted, and what would lift it.

## Rules with an exception

### Trace Filaments links its segments to parents itself (decided 2026-11-09)
Rule: only Relate Objects links objects (`Object::set_parent`, crate-only;
"new links only come from Relate Objects").
Exception: Trace Filaments (filaments, phase 2) creates one object per
segment of the traced tree and links each segment to the next segment
towards the soma itself.
Why: the tree is known exactly while tracing; Relate Objects would have to
infer it again from overlap, which fails for segments that touch or cross.
Effect: the segments' `parent_ids` come from the tracing, not from a Relate
step - a later Relate Objects on the same classes can still add links of
other parent classes.

## Whole slides: tiles without a halo

### Find Maxima, Watershed: a spot or split on a tile seam
Tile commands get no overlap with their neighbours. A peak exactly on a
seam can be found in both tiles (two spots, merged by the tile merge only
if their pixels touch); Watershed can split differently at a seam.
Small images are one tile - not affected.
Would lift it: tiles with a halo for the commands that need one.

### Vesselness: automatic noise level per tile
With "Noise level" 0 (automatic, Frangi's suggestion: half the strongest
structure) the level is computed per tile on a whole slide, so neighbouring
tiles can be scaled slightly differently. Small images are one tile - not
affected. A fixed noise level gives the same result on every tile.

## Approximations

### Measure Object Skeleton: longest path on skeletons with loops
The longest path is found with two sweeps along the skeleton - exact for a
tree, an approximation (a lower bound) when the skeleton has loops.

### Split view: the Sholl circles' centre
The results don't store the centre Measure Object Sholl used. The split
view draws the selected skeleton's circles around its parent on the image
(the soma Relate Objects linked it to), else around the skeleton itself -
the same centre when "Centre from" was the soma class Relate linked, an
approximation otherwise. The profile's values are not affected.

### Trace Filaments: crossing neurites (2D)
Where two neurites cross in the image, the cheapest path can turn off at
the crossing onto the other one - the known weakness of 2D tracers. A
direction-aware cost would lift it.

### Trace Filaments: competition within a soma's window
A tip goes to the soma that reaches it most cheaply among the somata in
the tracing soma's window (its box grown by the maximum reach). A soma
outside that window doesn't compete - with a reach smaller than the
distance between neighbouring neurons a tip near the window's edge can go
to the wrong one. A larger reach lifts it (more RAM per window).
